<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate the Altitude of a Right Triangle From Projections</h2>
  <p>Multiply the two hypotenuse projections, then take the square root.</p>
  <p>The Right Triangle Altitude From Projections Calculator finds the altitude of a right triangle from the two projections, <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />, that the altitude cuts on the hypotenuse. No legs or angles are needed. Enter projection <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and projection <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> in the same unit, and the calculator returns the altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> with the altitude theorem:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{p \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The formula works because dropping the altitude from the right-angle vertex creates two smaller triangles that are similar to each other. Similarity forces the altitude to be the geometric mean of the two projections. As a quick illustration, if <span set:html={rich('[[p=9\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=16\\text{ cm}]]')} />, multiply <span set:html={rich('[[9]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[16]]')} /> to get <span set:html={rich('[[144]]')} />, then take the square root to get an altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[12\\text{ cm}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Type the first hypotenuse segment into the Projection <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> field and the second into the Projection <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> field, keeping both in the same unit. Press Calculate to see the altitude, and Reset clears the fields for a new problem. The result carries the same unit as the inputs. If you know the triangle's legs instead, try the &lt;a href="/right-triangle-altitude-calculator/"&gt;altitude calculator&lt;/a&gt; built for that case.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-altitude -->
<section id="sec-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Altitude of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The altitude of a right triangle is the perpendicular segment drawn from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse. Its length measures the shortest distance from that vertex to the hypotenuse, because a perpendicular is always the shortest path from a point to a line.</p>
  <p>Draw the altitude and it lands on the hypotenuse at a point called the altitude foot, usually labeled D. From D, the altitude rises at exactly 90 degrees to meet the right-angle vertex C. This right-angle meeting is what makes CD the altitude rather than just another segment.</p>
  <p>Beginners sometimes confuse the altitude with one of the legs. The legs, AC and BC, meet at the right angle and form the outer sides of the triangle. The altitude CD runs inside the triangle from vertex C to side AB. It is a third, distinct segment with its own length <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />, and it exists only once the altitude is actually drawn.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse can serve as the base because any side can be a base once the matching altitude is drawn, letting the area be written as one half times the hypotenuse times this altitude. Drawing the altitude also splits the triangle into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, which carry the similar-triangle relationships behind every formula on this page.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-projections -->
<section id="sec-projections" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Are Projections in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>When the altitude CD meets the hypotenuse AB, the foot D divides AB into two smaller segments: AD, called <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} />, and DB, called <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />. These two segments are the projections of the legs onto the hypotenuse. Each one is the shadow a leg casts onto the hypotenuse when light shines straight down along the altitude.</p>
  <p>Projection <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> sits next to leg AC, and projection <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> sits next to leg BC. Because they are the two pieces of the hypotenuse, they add up exactly:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p+q=c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the full hypotenuse. This addition relationship helps check answers and recover a missing projection when the hypotenuse and one projection are known. If you need the reverse calculation, finding projections from side lengths, the &lt;a href="/right-triangle-projection-calculator/"&gt;projection calculator&lt;/a&gt; handles that direction.</p>
  <p>Both projections must be positive lengths. A zero or negative value describes no real triangle, only a degenerate shape. And projections are not the legs themselves: the legs AC and BC lean off to the sides, while <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> lie flat along the hypotenuse. Mixing them up is one of the most common errors students make.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Measurement</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">First hypotenuse segment</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Second hypotenuse segment</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Perpendicular altitude</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Complete hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">First triangle leg</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Second triangle leg</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Altitude From Projections Formula</h2>
  <p>The altitude theorem states:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h^2=p \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Taking the positive square root of both sides gives the working formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{p \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Each symbol has a fixed meaning. The letter <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> stands for the altitude to the hypotenuse, the quantity you want. The letters <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> stand for the two hypotenuse projections, the quantities you enter. The multiplication joins the projections, and the square root undoes that product to return a length.</p>
  <p>Multiplication appears because similarity between the two small triangles produces the proportion <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{h}{p}=\\frac{q}{h}]]')} />. Cross-multiplying that proportion gives <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=p\\times q]]')} />. The square root appears because <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is a length, and only the positive root of <span set:html={rich('[[h^2]]')} /> names a real distance.</p>
  <p>Units deserve attention. If <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> are both measured in centimeters, their product lives in square centimeters, which is correct for an area-like intermediate step. Taking the square root converts square centimeters back to plain centimeters for the final altitude. This is why the two inputs must share the same unit: mixing units would corrupt the product and the answer. A compact way to remember the formula is that the altitude is the geometric mean of the two projections, since the geometric mean of two numbers is the square root of their product.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-steps -->
<section id="sec-steps" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Altitude From Projections Step by Step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the two hypotenuse projections. Look for the segments into which the altitude foot divides the hypotenuse, and label the one next to leg AC as <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and the one next to leg BC as <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Confirm both measurements use the same unit. If one is in millimeters and the other in centimeters, convert one of them before touching the formula.</li>
    <li>Multiply <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />. The product <span set:html={rich('[[h^2]]')} /> is an area-like intermediate value, so its unit is the square of the input unit.</li>
    <li>Take the positive square root of the product. The negative root is discarded because a length cannot be negative.</li>
    <li>Write the final answer with its unit. A bare number invites mistakes later, especially when it feeds into a longer calculation.</li>
    <li>Check that the answer is reasonable. The altitude always lies between the two projections, and it can never exceed half the hypotenuse.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Here is the method applied to a full example. Suppose <span set:html={rich('[[p=4\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9\\text{ cm}]]')} />. First multiply: <span set:html={rich('[[4\\times 9=36]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=36\\text{ cm}^2]]')} />. Then take the positive square root:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{36}=6\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[6\\text{ cm}]]')} />. As a sanity check, <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} /> lies between the projections <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[9]]')} />, matching the geometric-mean behavior described later in this guide.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Altitude Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>Triangle ABC has its right angle at C. From C, the altitude CD drops perpendicular to the hypotenuse AB, meeting it at the foot D. The segment AD is labeled <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} />, DB is labeled <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />, and CD is labeled <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />, while the hypotenuse AB is <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and the legs AC and BC are <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Two small squares mark the two right angles: one at C inside the original triangle, and one at D where the altitude meets the hypotenuse. The altitude CD appears as a clean interior segment, visually distinct from the two legs that form the triangle's outer frame.</p>
  <p>The altitude divides ABC into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, which are similar to each other and to the original triangle. In textbook questions, projections are often described as the two segments of the hypotenuse, or simply as the numbers sitting on either side of the altitude foot. Label them <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> before computing anything.</p>
