    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-main.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with right angle at C, altitude CD to hypotenuse AB, projections AD = [[p]] and DB = [[q]], altitude CD = [[h]]" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Altitude CD splits hypotenuse AB into projections <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is the geometric mean of <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Altitude Formula Work? Proof Using Similar Triangles</h2>
  <p>The altitude theorem is not a rule to memorize blindly. It follows from the similarity of the two triangles the altitude creates.</p>
  <p>In triangle ACD, the acute angles at A and C add to 90 degrees, and in the large triangle ABC, angles A and B also add to 90 degrees. So the angle at C inside ACD equals angle B, and the angle at A equals the angle at C inside CBD. The two small triangles share all three angles, which makes them similar.</p>
  <p>Similarity means matching sides stay in the same ratio. In triangle ACD, the side opposite angle A is the altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />. In triangle CBD, the side opposite the equal angle at C is projection <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />. The other matching pair is projection <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> (opposite the second acute angle in ACD) over altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> (opposite the matching angle in CBD). Setting these equal gives the proportion:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{h}{p}=\\frac{q}{h}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Cross-multiplying turns the proportion into:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h^2=p \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Taking the square root of both sides gives <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{p\\times q}]]')} />. The equation <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=p\\times q]]')} /> has two algebraic roots, positive and negative, but a length is a positive quantity, so only the positive root describes the altitude.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-proof.svg')} alt="The two smaller right triangles ACD and CBD with corresponding sides labeled to show [[\frac{h}{p}=\frac{q}{h}]]" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangles ACD and CBD are similar, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{h}{p}=\\frac{q}{h}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> squared equals <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> times <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} />.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Altitude From Projections</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: Simple Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p=4\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9\\text{ cm}]]')} />, substitute into the formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{4 \\times 9}=\\sqrt{36}=6\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[6\\text{ cm}]]')} />, a whole number because <span set:html={rich('[[36]]')} /> is a perfect square.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Equal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p=5\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{5 \\times 5}=\\sqrt{25}=5\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>When the projections are equal, the altitude equals each projection: the isosceles right triangle case, where the altitude is exactly half the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Decimal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p=3.6\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=6.4\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{3.6 \\times 6.4}=\\sqrt{23.04}=4.8\\text{ m}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Decimals change nothing: <span set:html={rich('[[23.04]]')} /> is a perfect square, so the altitude is an exact <span set:html={rich('[[4.8\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Unequal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p=2\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=18\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{2 \\times 18}=\\sqrt{36}=6\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[6\\text{ cm}]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[10\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Averaging gives the arithmetic mean, <span set:html={rich('[[10]]')} />, but the altitude is the geometric mean, which runs lower for unequal inputs.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Non-Perfect Square</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p=5\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=7\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{5 \\times 7}=\\sqrt{35}\\text{ cm}\\approx 5.916\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[35]]')} /> is not a perfect square, so the exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{35}\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Use the <span set:html={rich('[[5.916\\text{ cm}]]')} /> approximation for practical work, but keep the exact form when the answer feeds further calculations.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Different Input Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p=40\\text{ mm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9\\text{ cm}]]')} />. The units differ, so convert first: <span set:html={rich('[[40\\text{ mm}=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Then:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{4 \\times 9}=\\sqrt{36}=6\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Converting first is the only safe move: feeding <span set:html={rich('[[40]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[9]]')} /> in directly gives <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{40\\times 9}=\\sqrt{360}]]')} />, a wrong and unitless number.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Your Altitude Calculation</h2>
  <h3>Method 1: Geometric Mean Check</h3>
  <p>Square your altitude and compare it with the product of the projections. The two values must match. For <span set:html={rich('[[p=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[6^2=36]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[4\\times 9=36]]')} />. Agreement confirms the arithmetic.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Hypotenuse and Leg Relationships</h3>
  <p>Rebuild the whole triangle and cross-check with a second formula. First add the projections: <span set:html={rich('[[c=4+9=13]]')} />. Then recover the legs with the projection-leg relations: <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{p\\times c}=\\sqrt{52}\\approx 7.211]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{q\\times c}=\\sqrt{117}\\approx 10.817]]')} />. Now verify with <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[(7.211\\times 10.817)/13\\approx 78/13=6]]')} />, which matches. A &lt;a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/"&gt;side calculator&lt;/a&gt; keeps the leg arithmetic tidy while you check.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Triangle Area Relationship</h3>
  <p>The same triangle has two expressions for its area. Using the legs: Area = <span set:html={rich('[[(a\\times b)/2=(7.211\\times 10.817)/2\\approx 39]]')} />. Using the hypotenuse and altitude: Area = <span set:html={rich('[[(c\\times h)/2=(13\\times 6)/2=39]]')} />. Both expressions agree, which independently confirms <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} />. The &lt;a href="/right-triangle-area-from-altitude-calculator/"&gt;area from altitude&lt;/a&gt; tool is built on this same relationship.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-relationships -->
<section id="sec-relationships" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Altitude, Hypotenuse, and Triangle Legs</h2>
  <p>Five formulas tie the six labeled parts together. The table below summarizes what each one finds and what it needs.</p>
  <p>The first four formulas need at least one projection. They come from the similar triangles the altitude creates, so they apply only when the altitude is drawn from the right-angle vertex. The fifth formula, <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} />, needs no projections at all: it comes from equating the two area expressions, and it works whenever the legs and hypotenuse are known.</p>
  <p>This is the practical difference between the two altitude calculators on this site. When you know the projections, the altitude theorem gives <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> directly in one step. When you know the sides instead, &lt;a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-sides-calculator/"&gt;finding altitude from the sides&lt;/a&gt; uses the area route. Pick whichever tool matches your given data.</p>
  <p>One more useful reading of <span set:html={rich('[[a^2=p\\times c]]')} />: each leg is the geometric mean of the hypotenuse and its adjacent projection, mirroring how the altitude is the geometric mean of the two projections.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Formula</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Finds</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Needs</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[c=p+q]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The hypotenuse</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Both projections</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[h^2=p\\times q]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The altitude</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Both projections</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[a^2=p\\times c]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The leg next to projection <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Projection <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[b^2=q\\times c]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The leg next to projection <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Projection <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> and the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The altitude</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Both legs and the hypotenuse</td></tr>
