</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-geomean -->
<section id="sec-geomean" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Geometric Mean Theorem and Right Triangle Altitude</h2>
  <p>The geometric mean of two positive numbers is the square root of their product. For <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> it is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{p\\times q}]]')} />, which is exactly the altitude formula. So saying the altitude is the geometric mean of the projections is just restating the theorem in different words.</p>
  <p>The arithmetic mean is the familiar average: <span set:html={rich('[[(p+q)/2]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[p=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9]]')} />, the geometric mean is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{36}=6]]')} /> while the arithmetic mean is <span set:html={rich('[[13/2=6.5]]')} />. The altitude equals <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[6.5]]')} />, because the similar-triangle proportion forces a multiplicative relationship, not an additive one.</p>
  <p>The geometric mean never exceeds the arithmetic mean for positive inputs, with equality only when <span set:html={rich('[[p=q]]')} />. That is why the altitude is largest, relative to the hypotenuse, when the projections are equal. A rectangle <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[9]]')} /> and a square <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} /> have the same area, <span set:html={rich('[[36]]')} />, which is the same idea drawn as shapes: the altitude is the side of the square whose area equals the rectangle's.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-geometric-mean.svg')} alt="Rectangle [[4]] by [[9]] and square [[6]] by [[6]] with equal area, plus a number line comparing geometric mean [[6]] and arithmetic mean [[6.5]]" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">For <span set:html={rich('[[p=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9]]')} />, the geometric mean <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} /> (the altitude) sits below the arithmetic mean <span set:html={rich('[[6.5]]')} />.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-special -->
<section id="sec-special" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Cases and Important Properties</h2>
  <h3>Equal Projections</h3>
  <p>When <span set:html={rich('[[p=q]]')} />, the altitude equals that common value: <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{p\\times p}=p]]')} />. The altitude foot lands at the hypotenuse midpoint, the legs are equal, and the triangle is an isosceles right triangle with 45-degree acute angles, giving the maximum altitude for a fixed hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Very Unequal Projections</h3>
  <p>When <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> is tiny and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> is huge, the altitude stays modest: <span set:html={rich('[[p=1]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=100]]')} /> give <span set:html={rich('[[h=10]]')} />, far below the projections' midpoint. The geometric mean weighs the small input more heavily than an average would.</p>
  <h3>Fixed Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Keep the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=p+q]]')} /> constant and vary the split. The product <span set:html={rich('[[p\\times q]]')} />, and therefore the altitude, peaks when the split is even. Algebraically, <span set:html={rich('[[h\\le\\frac{p+q}{2}]]')} />, with equality exactly when <span set:html={rich('[[p=q]]')} />. In plain terms, no altitude can exceed half its hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Zero Projections</h3>
  <p>Setting <span set:html={rich('[[p=0]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[h=0]]')} />: the altitude foot coincides with vertex A, the triangle collapses into a segment, and no genuine triangle remains. Treat zero projections as degenerate, not valid calculator input.</p>
  <h3>Negative Projections</h3>
  <p>A projection is a length, and lengths cannot be negative. A negative input has no geometric meaning and yields no valid altitude; the error lies upstream, in how the segment was measured.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-equal-vs-unequal.svg')} alt="Two right triangles with the same hypotenuse but different projection splits showing different altitudes" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same hypotenuse, different splits: equal projections give the tallest altitude.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Finding Altitude From Projections</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Adding the projections instead of multiplying them. The similar-triangle proportion requires a product: with <span set:html={rich('[[p=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{4+9}\\approx 3.606]]')} /> is wrong, while <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{4\\times 9}=6]]')} /> is correct.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the square root. Stopping at <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=36]]')} /> leaves an area-like number where a length belongs. Always take the positive square root to finish.</li>
    <li>Confusing projections with triangle legs. The legs lean off the hypotenuse while <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> lie flat along it; only the hypotenuse segments belong in the altitude theorem.</li>
    <li>Mixing units without converting. With <span set:html={rich('[[p=40\\text{ mm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=9\\text{ cm}]]')} />, convert first (<span set:html={rich('[[40\\text{ mm}=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />), then compute <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{4\\times 9}=6\\text{ cm}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Entering negative measurements. A negative projection is meaningless and can produce imaginary results. Confirm both <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> are positive before calculating.</li>
    <li>Carrying <span set:html={rich('[[h^2]]')} /> forward as <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> in longer solutions. Keep the labels straight: <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=36]]')} /> is the squared step, <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} /> is the altitude.</li>
    <li>Rounding too early. Keep exact forms like <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{35}]]')} /> as long as possible and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li>Applying the formula to a non-right triangle. The proportion <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{h}{p}=\\frac{q}{h}]]')} /> needs the right angle at C; without it, the similarity argument collapses and the formula does not hold.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of the Altitude Theorem</h2>
  <p>Geometry classes use this theorem constantly. Altitude problems test whether students can spot the projections, pick the right formula, and verify with the Pythagorean theorem, and textbook exercises often hand over the projections as plain numbers on a labeled diagram.</p>
  <p>In construction-style geometry problems, the theorem completes a right triangle when only partial measurements are known. Drafting a triangular truss may fix the hypotenuse segments first, with the altitude determining the peak height, and similarity proofs use the same <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{h}{p}=\\frac{q}{h}]]')} /> proportion directly.</p>
  <p>Surveying and engineering sketches sometimes model a sightline as the altitude of a right triangle, with the projections read off a baseline. Two arbitrary ground distances do not determine a height by themselves; the right-triangle geometry must genuinely hold before the theorem applies.</p>
  <p>Architectural drawings of gable ends or folded-plate roofs use the same relations to recover a missing dimension, provided the right angle is really there.</p>
</section>

